  research: I found several current frameworks and references that fit the “YAML-first LLM pipeline frameworks” theme. The space seems to split into two categories:

1. **True YAML-first orchestration frameworks** — workflows/pipelines are authored primarily in YAML.
2. **Declarative prompt/pipeline specs** — YAML is used heavily for prompts, steps, and configs, but the runtime may still rely on code.

## Key frameworks and projects

### 1) Microsoft Promptflow
- **What it is:** A framework for building executable LLM flows, with DAG flows defined in YAML.
- **Why it matters:** It’s one of the clearest examples of YAML-centered LLM pipeline authoring.
- **Evidence:** The docs state that a flow is “represented as a YAML file” and is “a DAG of functions” connected by input/output dependencies.
- **Source:**
  https://github.com/microsoft/promptflow/blob/main/docs/how-to-guides/develop-a-dag-flow/quick-start.md
  https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/machine-learning/prompt-flow/how-to-develop-flow?view=azureml-api-2

### 2) PDL (Prompt Declaration Language)
- **What it is:** A declarative language for reliable, composable LLM prompts and integration with traditional software systems.
- **Why it matters:** More prompt-spec than full pipeline orchestrator, but it is strongly declarative and YAML-based in community usage/discussion.
- **Evidence:** Official docs describe PDL as a declarative language for specifying prompt structure and composing LLM calls with rule-based systems.
- **Source:**
  https://ibm.github.io/prompt-declaration-language/
  https://github.com/IBM/prompt-declaration-language

### 3) YAMLGraph
- **What it is:** A declarative LLM pipeline orchestration framework where workflows are defined entirely in YAML.
- **Why it matters:** This is probably the most directly “YAML-first” project I found.
- **Evidence:** Project description says you can define complex AI workflows entirely in YAML, with support for routing, loops, agents, and human-in-the-loop.
- **Source:**
  https://pypi.org/project/yamlgraph/
  https://github.com/sheikkinen/yamlgraph
  https://sheikkinen.github.io/yamlgraph/

### 4) Langflow
- **What it is:** A visual AI workflow builder for LLM apps.
- **Why it matters:** Not strictly YAML-first, but it supports import/export of flows and is often used in declarative workflow discussions.
- **Source:**
  https://docs.langflow.org/
  https://docs.langflow.org/concepts-flows-import

### 5) Kestra
- **What it is:** General workflow orchestration, not LLM-specific, but increasingly relevant for AI/LLM pipelines.
- **Why it matters:** Their YAML-first workflow philosophy is directly applicable to LLM pipelines.
- **Source:**
  https://kestra.io/blogs/yaml-for-workflow-orchestration
  https://kestra.io/blogs/yaml-vs-python-workflow

## What the current landscape suggests

### YAML-first is appealing because:
- It is **version-controllable**
- It is **human-readable**
- It is easier to **generate, validate, and diff**
- It can make workflows more **portable** and **auditable**

### Typical YAML-first capabilities include:
- Prompt templates
- DAG or graph workflows
- Tool calls / function calls
- Conditional branching
- Loops / retries
- Human-in-the-loop steps
- Multi-model routing
- Evaluation or red-teaming configs

### Tradeoffs:
- YAML can become verbose for complex logic
- Some frameworks still require custom Python for advanced behavior
- Debugging and schema validation matter a lot
- Visual builders often complement YAML rather than replace it

## Best-fit interpretation of “YAML-first LLM pipeline frameworks”
If you mean frameworks where the **primary source of truth is YAML**, the strongest matches are:

- **Promptflow**
- **YAMLGraph**
- **PDL** (more prompt declaration than full pipeline orchestration)

If you mean broader orchestration stacks that are **YAML-friendly and AI-ready**, then:

- **Kestra**
- **Langflow**
- **DeepTeam** for YAML-based LLM red-teaming configs

